There have been myriad write-ups about how to accomplish this, but this is the most seamless and effective setup I have found thus far. This guide assumes that you have some sort of graphical desktop already up and running on your system.

<h3>Contents:</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Set up the Host</li>
  <li>Set up the Jail</li>
  <li>Custom Configuration</li>
</ul>

<h2>Set up the Host</h2>

<p>This process is fairly straightforward and just involves a couple configuration tweaks and package installations. For packages, we will install <code>pkg install doas linux_base-rl9 linux-rl9-vulkan linux-steam-utils pulseaudio</code>. Some of these may pull each other in or already come with your window manager.</p>

<p>Following this, a simple <code>sysrc linux_enable=yes</code> should do the trick.To make this take effect immediately, you can do <code>kldload linux.so; kldload linux64.so</code>.</p>

<h2>Set up the Jail</h2>

<h2>Custom Configuration</h2>

<h3>Notes:</h3>

<p>127.0.0.1 should be in your jail's address list, and your jail's primary IP should not be shared with any other jail.</p> <p>A FreeBSD jail without 127.0.0.1 in its address list has its loopback traffic rewritten to use the jail's primary address. Steam rejects this traffic because it appears to come from a non-loopback address. If you are getting persistent "Unexpected transport error" messages, this is likely the cause. FreeBSD only allows jails to share a single address from one family, so if your jail's primary IP is already being shared, you cannot share the loopback address.</p>
